\chapter{Cephyr object emission and C tools}
\label{ch:85}
\index{Cephyr object emission and C tools}

The optional Cephyr object mode consumes checked-HIR C11 and compiles within the process for its admitted AMD64 Linux profile. Request it through \OnxCodeTerm{--emit-cephyr-object} with an explicit output file in a compatible build. It produces an object for subsequent linkage and maintains its own semantic and target checks. The Cephyr standard-library and FFI module facilities are separately scoped.

\section{Keep object emission distinct from execution}
First inspect normal C output and define which symbols the object should provide. Link it against the required matching runtime and run an execution oracle with the same inputs as the reference build. Test rejection of an incompatible target and preserve any verification report. If a generated object needs a provider from the FFI-module profile, check the provider evidence and loader restrictions as well as the object symbol. A successful object build does not exercise a dynamic module lifecycle or certify a foreign ABI.

\section{Read a three-link ABI experiment}
The clamp probe asks whether a generated scalar adapter agrees with the
real kernel declaration on native AMD64 Linux. Give the adapter and the
kernel their own translation units. First compile both with the host C
compiler, link the oracle, and establish the expected boundary behavior.
Next compile just the adapter with embedded Cephyr and keep the host-built
kernel. Finally use independently Cephyr-built objects for both. Each
link contains one kernel implementation. The recorded probe runs the same
2,670 comparisons in each arrangement; changing the oracle between links
would obscure which object substitution changed the result.

A deliberately wrong prototype should fail the declaration check before
the numerical oracle runs. Compare this to the historical failure in the
real declaration header: preprocessing stopped at an unrelated include
before the adapter's assertions even ran. Removing that unnecessary
dependency let the probe reach the actual ABI question. A passing scalar
oracle provides evidence about this representation and linkage, not about
unreviewed contracts or the lifetime of a symbol obtained from a loaded
module.

When diagnosing a new failure, distinguish an object from the report
about its compilation. A Cephyr diagnostic may name a generated C line
and relate it to an enclosing On1x statement; its C column is not an
original source token position. SARIF and object publication are separate.
An existing object beside a fresh diagnostic can be yesterday's object.
Check the invocation status before interpreting that pair as a successful
new generation.

